      "id": "mentalhealth-20260924",
      "section": "science",
      "fly": "Measuring care",
      "title": "Grading the counsellor",
      "rubric": "More than a million people a week discuss suicide with ChatGPT. OpenAI has built a test of how chatbots respond, and the best score is 57%",
      "date": "2026-09-28",
      "eventDate": "2026-09-24",
      "editionId": "2026-09-28",
      "kind": "Analysis",
      "body": [
        "In October 2025 OpenAI estimated that in any given week about 0.15% of ChatGPT’s users had conversations containing explicit signs of suicidal planning or intent. With some 800m weekly users at the time, that was more than a million people. How well chatbots respond to them has been judged mostly by anecdote: a transcript that went wrong here, a grateful user there. On September 24th OpenAI published an attempt at something more systematic. MentalHealthBench consists of 1,215 synthetic conversations, graded against 5,262 criteria written by more than 80 licensed psychologists and psychiatrists from 22 countries, in 19 languages.",
        "The cases are weighted towards difficulty. Just over half (53.5%) are non-acute; 18.2% are high-acuity and 28.3% are emergencies. On OpenAI’s figures, its own GPT-6 Astra scored best, at 57.3%, followed by GPT-6 Sol at 53.9% and Anthropic’s Claude Opus 5.5 at 52.4%. Older models did much worse: GPT-4o scored 32.1% and Google’s Gemini 2.5 Pro 29.5%.",
        {
          "chart": "mhb-mix"
        },
        {
          "chart": "mhb-scores"
        },
        "Three cautions apply. The conversations are synthetic, written to resemble real exchanges rather than drawn from them. The benchmark comes from a company whose models top it, which is common in AI but not reassuring. And a percentage of rubric criteria met is not the same as a measure of harm avoided. A reply can tick most boxes and still miss the one that matters, such as recognising that a user is in danger.",
        "Even so, a public rubric is progress. It turns vague promises about “safety” into specific, checkable behaviours that others can test, dispute and improve. The headline result is sobering, not triumphant: the best model meets little more than half of what clinicians say a good response should contain. That is a reason to keep building the measure, and to keep human help close at hand. ■"
      ],
      "sources": [
        {
          "title": "OpenAI: Introducing MentalHealthBench",
          "url": "https://openai.com/index/introducing-mentalhealthbench/",
          "date": "2026-09-24"
        },
        {
          "title": "OpenAI: Strengthening ChatGPT’s responses in sensitive conversations",
          "url": "https://openai.com/index/strengthening-chatgpt-responses-in-sensitive-conversations/",
          "date": "2025-10-27"
        }
      ],
      "brief": "OpenAI released MentalHealthBench on September 24th: 1,215 synthetic conversations graded against criteria written by more than 80 clinicians. Its GPT-6 Astra scored highest, at 57.3%. The test is synthetic and was built by a company whose models lead it.",
      "image": "mentalhealth-20260924",
      "minutes": 2,
      "format": "news-analysis"
