      "id": "yandex-20261011",
      "section": "business",
      "fly": "Infrastructure at war",
      "title": "Data centres in the crosshairs",
      "rubric": "Drone strikes have knocked out three of Yandex’s computing sites in four days. AI’s physical footprint is now a military target",
      "date": "2026-10-12",
      "eventDate": "2026-10-11",
      "editionId": "2026-10-12",
      "kind": "Briefing",
      "body": [
        "For most of its history the internet has been imagined as weightless: a cloud, a web, a space. Its physical reality, of buildings full of humming machines connected to power stations and fibre-optic cables, rarely featured in strategic thinking. That is changing. On October 11th Yandex, Russia’s largest technology company, said its data centre in the Vladimir region had been “completely shut down” after a drone attack. No one was injured. It was the third of the firm’s computing sites to be hit in four days.",
        "The sequence began on October 8th, when a fire after an attack cut power to a data centre in Sasovo, in the Ryazan region. Reports said the site housed two of the three supercomputers Yandex uses to train its AI models. On October 9th several modules at a facility in Kaluga were knocked out of operation. Then came Vladimir, which hosts one of the availability zones of Yandex Cloud; customers there reported power disruptions. Yandex said services were gradually resuming, but in emergency mode.",
        {
          "chart": "yandex-map"
        },
        {
          "h": "Who and why"
        },
        "Responsibility has not been formally claimed. Volodymyr Zelensky, Ukraine’s president, said only that his country was responding to Russian attacks. Ukraine has spent much of the war striking Russian refineries, depots and power infrastructure with long-range drones, and computing facilities fit a broader logic. Yandex runs services that Russians use daily, from taxis to payments, and develops AI models used across the Russian economy. Its data centres also host the cloud infrastructure that many organisations, including some state bodies, depend on.",
        "From a military perspective, data centres are attractive targets. They are large, fixed and hard to hide. They need enormous, continuous power, so damaging the electrical supply can be as effective as hitting the building. Their contents are expensive and slow to replace: advanced chips are scarce in Russia, which faces Western export controls and must import them through intermediaries. A destroyed AI supercomputer may take years to rebuild. That makes such strikes unusually damaging to a country’s long-term technological ambitions, not just its short-term services.",
        "The disruption also shows how much of daily life now runs through a few buildings. When one Yandex zone lost power, the effects reportedly reached taxi bookings, payments and cloud services used by other businesses. Companies that had never thought of themselves as war targets discovered that their software was hosted in one. That is the nature of shared infrastructure: its efficiency comes from pooling, and so does its fragility.",
        {
          "h": "A lesson for everyone else"
        },
        "The implications extend far beyond Russia. Governments everywhere now describe AI computing capacity as a strategic asset, comparable to energy or ports. America, China, the Gulf states and Europe are pouring money into ever larger data centres. Those facilities are concentrated where land, power and cooling are cheap, which often means a small number of sites. Concentration brings efficiency. It also creates single points of failure.",
        "The scale of investment makes the vulnerability more acute. Technology firms are projected to spend trillions of dollars on AI and data centres by the end of the decade. China alone is planning vast new grid links to carry wind and solar power from inland provinces to coastal computing hubs, according to BloombergNEF. Each of these assets will need defending, not only against hackers but against physical attack, sabotage and the disruption of the power and cables they depend on.",
        {
          "h": "Resilience by design"
        },
        "Cloud providers have long designed for failure. They split their services across availability zones, separate buildings with independent power, so that losing one does not take down a service. Yandex’s experience suggests that such designs, built for accidents, may not withstand deliberate, repeated attack on several sites within days. Training AI models is especially exposed, because a large training run may depend on thousands of chips working together in one place for months.",
        "Expect three responses. The first is dispersal: spreading computing across more sites, including smaller ones, at some cost in efficiency. The second is hardening: physical defences, air defence near critical facilities and backup power that can run for long periods. The third is insurance and law. Insurers will price war risk into data-centre cover, and governments may designate computing facilities as critical infrastructure with the protections and obligations that brings.",
        "There are also questions of law. Attacks on civilian infrastructure are constrained by the laws of war, which require that targets make an effective contribution to military action and that harm to civilians be proportionate. Data centres are often dual-use: the same building may host a bank’s systems, a hospital’s records and a defence ministry’s software. Deciding which side of the line such facilities fall on will be contentious, and the answer may differ from one data hall to the next. Operators may come under pressure to separate military from civilian workloads, as some telecoms firms already do with their networks.",
        "None of this is new for power stations or refineries. What is new is that the brains of the digital economy have joined them on the list of things that can be bombed. The war in Ukraine has repeatedly served as a laboratory for the future of conflict, from cheap drones to electronic warfare. This week it added another lesson. As AI becomes central to economic and military power, the buildings in which it lives become targets too. ■"
      ],
